cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Reported accuracy in geohazard susceptibility mapping is a function of the
  absence sampling design
message: >-
  If you use this software or build on these results, please cite the work
  using the metadata below.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Sanskar
    family-names: Gupta
    email: Sanskar.in127@gmail.com
    affiliation: >-
      School of Computer Science Engineering and Technology, Bennett
      University, Greater Noida, India
repository-code: 'https://github.com/sanskarprime392-png/avalanche'
abstract: >-
  Susceptibility maps for geohazards are built from presence-only data, so the
  absence class is constructed by the analyst. This work shows in simulation
  that a model with no predictive skill reports an area under the ROC curve of
  0.739 at one standard deviation of absence displacement and 1.000 at six,
  and demonstrates by construction that the absolute skill of a presence-only
  model is not identifiable from the data it was fitted to. What remains
  identifiable is the relative effect of the absence design, measured by
  holding presences, predictors and model fixed and varying only the sampling.
  An independent Sentinel-1 avalanche inventory for the Chandra-Bhaga and
  Upper Beas basins of the Western Himalaya provides the empirical test.
keywords:
  - susceptibility mapping
  - pseudo-absence selection
  - sampling bias
  - model evaluation
  - presence-only data
  - snow avalanches
  - Sentinel-1
license: MIT
